{%- comment -%}
  Phase 8. Horizon loads roughly thirty module scripts on every template. Each one
  registers custom elements; scripts/audit-scripts.mjs fetches every template from
  the build store and reports which of those elements actually appear. These five
  matched nothing anywhere, are imported by no other module, and have no dynamic
  path in, so they were removed:

    - layered-slideshow.js — sections/layered-slideshow.liquid — not used by any template
    - video-background.js — Horizon media sections — syp-hero uses video_tag, not video-background-component
    - volume-pricing.js — quantity-break block — absent from templates/product.json
    - price-per-item.js — quantity-break block — absent from templates/product.json
    - volume-pricing-info.js — quantity-break block — absent from templates/product.json.
      Note this one still arrives sitewide via snippets/cart-products.liquid, which loads
      it for the cart drawer; removing it here only stopped the duplicate.

  Everything reachable through the quick-add dialog was deliberately kept. quick-add.js
  fetches the product page's HTML and injects it, and module scripts inside injected
  markup do not execute — so variant-picker, product-form, product-price,
  quantity-selector and media-gallery must stay loaded sitewide even though the
  audit shows their elements only on /products. Gating those to the product
  template would break add-to-cart from a collection page.

  An unregistered custom element fails silently rather than throwing, so re-add the
  matching script here if a template ever starts rendering one of these blocks, and
  re-run `node scripts/audit-scripts.mjs` after any template change.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- comment -%}
  Phase 8, found by scripts/check-templates.mjs. sections/syp-header.liquid renders
  a <header-actions> element with the `ref="liveRegion"` and the
  aria-controls="cart-drawer" trigger that Horizon's HeaderActions component
  requires — but header-actions.js only ever loaded from snippets/header-actions.liquid,
  which belongs to Horizon's own header and this theme does not render. So the element
  sat in the DOM unupgraded on every page since Phase 1: the cart count was never
  announced to screen readers and the cart button's aria-expanded never changed.
  An undefined custom element fails silently, which is why nothing surfaced it.
{%- endcomment -%}
